    <PostSection id="formal" no="04" label="fv-toolkit" title="Some claims need more than tests">
      <p class="lede">
        A test tries the cases someone thought of. A claim that says
        <em>never</em> or <em>always</em> is about the cases nobody thought of.
      </p>
      <p>
        For those, a model checker such as TLC or the Alloy analyzer tries
        every ordering, or every small structure, of a cut-down version of the
        system.<sup><a href="#src-7">7</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-8">8</a></sup>
        The fv-toolkit<sup><a href="#src-11">11</a></sup> decides when that is
        worth it. Most features have no such claim, and its first job is to say
        so in one paragraph.
      </p>

      <h3>In one picture</h3>
      <figure class="post-panel" aria-label="Seven shapes of claim and the check each one gets. Orderings get a TLA+ model. Which configurations can exist together get an Alloy model. A finite decision function gets every input through the real entry point. A function over unbounded inputs gets properties on generated inputs. Two paths that must agree get a differential test. Arithmetic gets property tests on the real code. Data that acts as the program gets checks on the shipped data.">
        <figcaption class="chart-title">Pick a claim. See the check it gets.</figcaption>
        <div class="tiles">
          {claimShapes.map((c, i) => (
            <details class="explorer-card" name="claims" open={i === 0}>
              <summary><b>&ldquo;{claimExamples[i]}&rdquo;</b><span>{c.shape}</span></summary>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>The check</dt><dd>{c.check}</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Why a weaker check fails</dt><dd>{c.why}</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Skill</dt><dd>{c.skill}</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </details>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="fig-note">
          The seven shapes, checks and reasons are the toolkit&rsquo;s. The
          example sentences are mine.
        </p>
      </figure>

      <h3>What sets it apart</h3>
      <div class="tiles">
        {fvTiles.map((t) => (
          <details class="explorer-card" name="fv-tiles">
            <summary><b>{t.title}</b></summary>
            <p>{t.body}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
      <figure class="post-panel" aria-label={`The tools behind the router. Core: ${tools.filter((t) => t.core).map((t) => t.name).join(", ")}. Installed on demand: ${tools.filter((t) => !t.core).map((t) => t.name).join(", ")}.`}>
        <figcaption class="chart-title">The tool shelf</figcaption>
        <ul class="shelf">
          {tools.map((t) => <li class={t.core ? "core" : ""}>{t.name}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="fig-note">
          Three are core. The rest install the first time the router names
          them. Apalache shares the TLA+ skill. Each tool&rsquo;s question is in
          the explorer below, under Tools.
        </p>
      </figure>

      <h3>Watch it work</h3>
      <p>
        An eval hands the RFC stage a draft design for holds on gift vouchers.
        I wrote the draft with a flaw in it and didn&rsquo;t say where. The
        spec&rsquo;s claim: the total captured against a voucher never exceeds
        its face value.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-panel" aria-label="The draft design in three steps. Hold: in one transaction the till checks the available amount, subtracts it and inserts a live hold that expires in ten minutes. Capture: when the sale completes, the till sets its hold to captured by the hold's id and adds the amount to the voucher's captured total. Sweeper: every minute, each live hold that has expired is released and its amount returned.">
        <figcaption class="chart-title">The draft design</figcaption>
        <ol class="design">
          <li><b>Hold.</b> In one transaction, the till reads what&rsquo;s available. If it covers the amount, the till subtracts it and inserts a <code>live</code> hold that expires in ten minutes.</li>
          <li><b>Capture.</b> When the sale completes, the till sets its hold to <code>captured</code> by the hold&rsquo;s id, and adds the amount to the voucher&rsquo;s <code>captured</code> total.</li>
          <li><b>Sweeper.</b> Every minute, each <code>live</code> hold that has expired is set to <code>released</code>, and its amount goes back to the voucher.</li>
        </ol>
      </figure>
      <Reveal summary="Lock in your answer. Can this voucher be captured above its face value?">
        <p>
          <b>Yes, in seven steps.</b> TLC found them in a model of the draft,
          with two tills, a voucher worth 5 and holds of 3. Step through it.
        </p>
        <Stepper
          id="counterexample"
          title="The order of steps the checker found"
          label="Seven steps on a voucher worth 5. Till A holds 3. The hold expires while the sale is still running. The sweeper releases it. Till A's sale completes and captures the released hold, so captured is 3. Till B holds 3 and captures. Captured is 6 on a voucher worth 5."
          steps={trace}
        />
        <p>
          The fix is one condition. Capture changes a hold only where its state
          is still <code>live</code>, and adds to the total only when a row
          changed. The stage wrote that into the RFC and left the approval to a
          person.
        </p>
      </Reveal>

      <p>
        A model that passes is easy to produce and hard to trust. So the fixed
        design&rsquo;s pass came back with four receipts.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-panel" aria-label="A pass with its receipts. The chosen design holds. The first draft is kept as a config that must fail, and it does. Two witness configs show that a capture and a sweeper release can be reached. Two planted bugs were detected.">
        <figcaption class="chart-title">A pass you can check</figcaption>
        <div class="receipts">
          <div><p class="receipt-k">Holds</p><p><code>CaptureLiveOnly.cfg</code></p><p>The chosen design keeps the property.</p></div>
          <div><p class="receipt-k">Must fail, and does</p><p><code>CaptureByIdOnly.cfg</code></p><p>The first draft stays in the folder as a config that has to break the property.</p></div>
          <div><p class="receipt-k">Reachable</p><p><code>WitnessCapture.cfg</code>, <code>WitnessRelease.cfg</code></p><p>A capture and a release can really happen in the model.</p></div>
          <div><p class="receipt-k">2 planted bugs caught</p><p><code>fv-mutate</code></p><p>A third plant turned out harmless under the fix, so it was dropped.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p class="fig-note">
          Bounds: two tills, one hold each, face value 5, amount 3. The run&rsquo;s
          own file calls it &ldquo;a bounded pass, not a proof.&rdquo;
        </p>
      </figure>

      <p>
        A design can be right and the code can still depart from it. So the
        Test stage sends a subagent to model the code. It gets the spec and the
        confirmed properties as sentences. It&rsquo;s told not to read the RFC,
        the plan or the first model.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-panel" aria-label="The same property checked twice. The design model, written at the RFC stage, holds. The code model, written in the Test stage by a subagent that never saw the design model, fails with two causes: capture never checks the hold's state, and place_hold reads the available amount and then writes it separately.">
        <figcaption class="chart-title">Two models that share no assumptions</figcaption>
        <div class="cols">
          <div class="col">
            <p class="col-score">holds</p>
            <h4>The design, modelled at RFC</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Written from <code>rfc.md</code>, before any code</li>
              <li>Capture needs a <code>live</code> hold</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="col col-after">
            <p class="col-score">fails</p>
            <h4>The code, modelled at Test</h4>
            <ul>
              <li><code>capture()</code> never checks the hold&rsquo;s state. Called twice, it books 10,000 on a 5,000 voucher.</li>
              <li><code>place_hold()</code> reads, then writes. Two tills each read 5,000 and hold 8,000 between them.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="fig-note">
          I planted the first flaw in the fixture&rsquo;s code. I didn&rsquo;t
          plant the second. Two of the three runs reported it, and one
          reproduced it with two threads and a real SQLite file.
        </p>
      </figure>

      <h3>Every pattern it encodes</h3>
      <p>
        The catalog runs from an architecture pattern to the check that fits
        it. Search for something you run in production, such as
        &ldquo;saga&rdquo;, &ldquo;cache&rdquo; or &ldquo;webhook&rdquo;.
      </p>
      <Explorer
        id="fv-explorer"
        title="fv-toolkit: tools, 166 patterns and eval scenarios"
        label="A browsable list of the fv-toolkit's 17 tool rows, its 166 architecture patterns in seven catalogs and its 38 eval scenarios. Each pattern card opens to what breaks, the tool, the model, the properties and the link to code."
        groups={fvGroups}
        cards={fvCards}
        searchHint="Search all 221 cards"
      />

      <h3>How it was measured</h3>
      <dl class="numbers" aria-label="fv-toolkit eval results: 29 of 29 routing cases, 5 of 5 cases that run a real checker on a planted bug, and 4 of 4 cases where it stays out.">
        <div><dt>29 of 29</dt><dd>routing cases went to the fitting tool and no other</dd></div>
        <div><dt>5 of 5</dt><dd>cases ran a real checker on a planted bug and reported the counterexample</dd></div>
        <div><dt>4 of 4</dt><dd>cases where it stayed out: a git question, a unit test, a type error, a flaky CI job</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="note">
        What these don&rsquo;t show: one sample per case, so no measure of
        variance.<sup><a href="#src-11">11</a></sup> And a pass is bounded by
        the sizes that were checked. It&rsquo;s never a proof.
      </p>
    </PostSection>

    <PostSection id="review" no="05" label="pr-review" title="A reviewer with no context">
      <p class="lede">
        The session that wrote a change is the worst reader of it, because it
        reads what it meant. My <a href="/blog/inattentional-blindness/">last
        post</a> was about people doing the same thing.
      </p>

      <h3>In one picture</h3>
      <figure class="post-panel" aria-label="An illustration. The session that wrote a change reads the diff with its own reasoning in mind. A fresh session has only the diff and the repo's rules, so it has to check what the first one assumes.">
        <figcaption class="chart-title">Same diff, two readers</figcaption>
        <div class="cols">
          <div class="col">
            <h4>The session that wrote it</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>&ldquo;I renamed it in all six places.&rdquo;</li>
              <li>&ldquo;That guard was redundant.&rdquo;</li>
              <li>&ldquo;The tests passed a minute ago.&rdquo;</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="col col-after">
            <h4>A session that starts cold</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>What does this diff change?</li>
              <li>What do the repo&rsquo;s review rules say?</li>
              <li>What breaks if this line is wrong?</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="fig-note">An illustration, not a measurement.</p>
      </figure>

      <h3>What sets it apart</h3>
      <div class="tiles">
        {reviewTiles.map((t) => (
          <details class="explorer-card" name="review-tiles">
            <summary><b>{t.title}</b></summary>
            <p>{t.body}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

      <h3>Watch it work</h3>
      <figure class="composer post-panel" aria-label="The panel says who reviews: by default three senior engineers and a tech lead, or two attackers and a defender with the security red-team panel. Each lens adds a reviewer or a rule on top: a migrations rule, an accessibility checklist, or an over-engineering reviewer. The severity scale, fingerprints and output format are the same in every combination.">
        <figcaption class="chart-title">Compose a review</figcaption>
        <div class="composer-controls">
          <fieldset>
            <legend>Panel</legend>
            <label><input type="radio" name="panel" id="panel-default" checked /> Default</label>
            <label><input type="radio" name="panel" id="panel-red" /> Security red team</label>
          </fieldset>
          <fieldset>
            <legend>Lenses</legend>
            <label><input type="checkbox" id="lens-migrations" /> Migrations</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox" id="lens-a11y" /> Accessibility</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox" id="lens-ponytail" /> Ponytail</label>
          </fieldset>
        </div>
        <ul class="reviewers" aria-live="polite">
          <li class="rev-default">Senior engineer</li>
          <li class="rev-default">Senior engineer</li>
          <li class="rev-default">Senior engineer</li>
          <li class="rev-default">Tech lead <small>checks their findings against the wider codebase</small></li>
          <li class="rev-red">Attacker</li>
          <li class="rev-red">Attacker</li>
          <li class="rev-red">Defender</li>
          <li class="rev-lens rev-migrations">+ Rule <small>every migration is reversible</small></li>
          <li class="rev-lens rev-a11y">+ Accessibility checklist</li>
          <li class="rev-lens rev-ponytail">+ Over-engineering reviewer <small>its findings are never High or Critical</small></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="fig-note">
          A panel and a lens are each one markdown file. The severity scale,
          the fingerprints and the output format stay the same in every
          combination.<sup><a href="#src-12">12</a></sup>
        </p>
      </figure>

      <figure class="post-panel" aria-label="A finding keeps one fingerprint across runs. A fingerprint that appears is new, one in two runs in a row is open, and one that disappears is fixed.">
        <figcaption class="chart-title">One finding across three runs</figcaption>
        <p class="fingerprint"><code>PERF|bin/invoices|csv|one-awk-process-per-call</code></p>
        <ol class="runs">
          <li><span>Run 1</span><b>new</b></li>
          <li><span>Run 2</span><b>open</b></li>
          <li><span>Run 3</span><b class="fixed">fixed</b></li>
        </ol>
        <p class="fig-note">
          An illustration of the rule the loop applies after each run. A stage
