      <h3>How it was measured</h3>
      <p>
        It hasn&rsquo;t been. No eval has loaded pr-review. Its script has a
        test that stubs <code>claude</code> and <code>gh</code>, which checks
        the plumbing and says nothing about how good a review
        is.<sup><a href="#src-12">12</a></sup>
      </p>
    </PostSection>

    <PostSection id="guarantees" no="06" label="Guarantees" title="A step can’t be skipped quietly">
      <p class="lede">
        Loading text in layers has a cost. The agent can decide a layer
        isn&rsquo;t worth loading. Mid-build I typed what I wanted instead:
        &ldquo;basically have progressive disclosure but guarantees of
        usage.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <p>
        The guarantee is a ledger. Each stage has a list of tools it must
        account for. In the feature&rsquo;s <code>sdlc.yaml</code>, each tool
        maps to the file it produced or to a skip reason. A hook reads that
        file before every commit. Tick the last box, then untick another.
      </p>

      <figure class="ledger post-panel" aria-label="The gate hook denies a commit that approves a stage while one of the stage's tools has neither a file nor a skip reason. The Test stage requires four tools: testing, test-audit, verifier and pr-review.">
        <figcaption class="chart-title">Approve the Test stage</figcaption>
        <div class="ledger-tools">
          <label><input type="checkbox" id="tool-testing" checked /> <code>testing</code> <span>testing/test.md</span></label>
          <label><input type="checkbox" id="tool-audit" checked /> <code>test-audit</code> <span>testing/audit.md</span></label>
          <label><input type="checkbox" id="tool-verifier" checked /> <code>verifier</code> <span>verify.md</span></label>
          <label><input type="checkbox" id="tool-review" /> <code>pr-review</code> <span>&ldquo;skipped: not installed&rdquo;</span></label>
        </div>
        <p class="ledger-out ledger-allow" aria-live="polite"><b>git commit: allowed.</b> Every tool is on record.</p>
        <p class="ledger-out ledger-deny" aria-live="polite">
          <b>git commit: denied.</b> &ldquo;The sdlc loop approves a stage only
          when each tool it requires is on record.
          <span class="miss miss-testing">test needs testing in docs/sdlc/csv-export/sdlc.yaml.</span>
          <span class="miss miss-audit">test needs test-audit in docs/sdlc/csv-export/sdlc.yaml.</span>
          <span class="miss miss-verifier">test needs verifier in docs/sdlc/csv-export/sdlc.yaml.</span>
          <span class="miss miss-review">test needs pr-review in docs/sdlc/csv-export/sdlc.yaml.</span>
          Under the stage&rsquo;s <code>tools:</code>, map each one to the file it
          produced, or to &ldquo;skipped: &lt;reason&gt;&rdquo; when the approver
          skipped it or it isn&rsquo;t installed. Then commit again.&rdquo;
        </p>
        <p class="fig-note">
          The message is the hook&rsquo;s own. A feature whose design was
          modelled owes a fifth tool here: <code>fv-verifier</code>.
        </p>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The hook has two other rules. It denies a commit on the default
        branch, and it makes Claude Code ask me before a PR is approved or
        merged.<sup><a href="#src-9">9</a></sup>
      </p>

      <h3>What the evals caught</h3>
      <p>
        After the first eval run I wrote: &ldquo;these cases seem kinda biased
        and not really a representative set.&rdquo; So the suite got wider.
        Later a second suite loaded the toolkits for real, and its first runs
        found the first two files below.
      </p>
      <div class="case-grid">
        <CaseFile
          no="01"
          name="Build wrote the tests by hand"
          staring="the tests the plan had already named"
          invisible="the planted-bug check and the report, which never happened"
          seenBy="a grader that looks for the call to the toolkit"
          catcher="One rule."
          question="What changed?"
        >
          <p>
            The plan named every test, so the Build stage wrote them itself
            and never called the testing toolkit. The tests looked fine. But
            nothing had planted a bug to watch them fail, and no report was
            saved. The case scored 0.61.
          </p>
          <Fragment slot="verdict">When a step names a skill or a subagent, the
stage invokes it. Work done by hand now counts as a skipped tool in the
ledger, with that reason. The case scored 0.99 over the next three runs.</Fragment>
        </CaseFile>

        <CaseFile
          no="02"
          name="The stage ended while its subagent was still working"
          staring="a subagent that had been dispatched"
          invisible="a run with nobody at the keyboard ends when the turn ends"
          seenBy="a report file that didn’t exist"
          catcher="One sentence."
          question="What changed?"
        >
          <p>
            The Test stage sent the subagent to model the code, then ended its
            turn. In a session with nobody at the keyboard, that ends the run,
            and the subagent stops with it. The report never appeared. The
            case scored 0.48.
          </p>
          <Fragment slot="verdict">The stage now waits until the report exists
before it ends its turn. The case scored 0.98 over the next three runs.</Fragment>
        </CaseFile>

        <CaseFile
          no="03"
          name="The fixture could have taught the answer"
          staring="one score"
          invisible="the fixture and the skill’s own example shared a domain"
          seenBy="reading the fixture beside the skill"
          catcher="A new fixture."
          question="What changed?"
        >
          <p>
            One eval fixture was a checkout page with a cart. The example
            inside the skill it tested is also a checkout with a cart. A run
            on that fixture can&rsquo;t tell an agent that applied the rule
            from one that copied the example.
          </p>
          <Fragment slot="verdict">The fixture became the booking page of a
tennis club, and no word of it appears in the skill. The gap stayed, so it
was real, and the skill changed. Every fixture now uses a domain that no
skill example uses.</Fragment>
        </CaseFile>
      </div>

      <h3>How the loop was measured</h3>
      <dl class="numbers" aria-label="Loop eval results: 0.99 with the plugin and 0.83 without over 17 cases, and the toolkit's skill called in 15 of 15 runs with a mean of 0.99 over five cases with the toolkits loaded.">
        <div><dt>0.99 <small>vs 0.83</small></dt><dd>17 cases with only the loop loaded, with the plugin and without, three runs each</dd></div>
        <div><dt>15 of 15</dt><dd>runs in which a stage called the toolkit it was meant to call</dd></div>
        <div><dt>0.99</dt><dd>mean of the five cases that load the toolkits, three runs each</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="note">
        Claude Sonnet 5.5 was the model under test and the
        judge.<sup><a href="#src-9">9</a></sup> The five-case suite has no
        run without the plugin to compare with.
      </p>
    </PostSection>

