    <PostSection id="limits" no="08" label="Limits" title="What isn’t proven yet">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>No real feature has gone through the loop.</b> Every example on
          this page is a scripted run on a made-up repo of a few files.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Four helpers have never been loaded in an eval.</b> EGM,
          pr-review, ponytail and my rfc skill. Nothing here measures a
          Goldfish review, a code review or a written
          RFC.<sup><a href="#src-9">9</a></sup>
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>The samples are small.</b> Three runs per case for the loop, and
          one run per case for the two toolkits.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Three graders failed in one run each</b> in the last full run with
          the toolkits. In one Build run, the tests and the code landed in one
          commit. In one Plan run, the judge voted two to one that a row had no
          kind. In one run, the stage skipped a check of the pinned properties.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>The hook has a known hole.</b> It reads the working tree before a
          command runs. One command that edits <code>sdlc.yaml</code> and
          commits isn&rsquo;t checked.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>The reviews are heavy on purpose.</b> A Goldfish review starts
          several subagents, and each code review is a full session. I
          don&rsquo;t yet know what a real feature costs end to end.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Next I use it on real work. When I have, each stop in section 01 gets
        an &ldquo;In real use&rdquo; note, and this section gets shorter or
        longer.
      </p>
    </PostSection>

